{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfcUNm9KxzmTjhnNkJMyZ", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7608, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16975, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7608}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "75847d75-5011-4128-88c9-9a48b5624415", "uuid": "b797b352-71ff-45ec-97ea-2acdcfa73aed", "timestamp": "2026-10-02T00:35:13.080Z", "request_id": "req_011CfcUNkwgBN9VeBWPwYJLn", "thinking_duration_ms": 229}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfcUNm9KxzmTjhnNkJMyZ", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "`git rebase --onto` lets you choose **where** commits get replayed, separately from **which** commits get replayed.\n\n## Syntax\n\n```\ngit rebase --onto <newbase> <upstream> [<branch>]\n```\n\nGit takes the commits that are in `<branch>` but not in `<upstream>`, which is the range `upstream..branch`. It then replays them on top of `<newbase>`.\n\nA plain `git rebase <upstream>` uses `<upstream>` both to pick the commits and as the new base. `--onto` lets those two be different.\n\n## Example: move a branch off another feature branch\n\nYou branched `feature-b` from `feature-a`, but `feature-b` doesn't actually need anything from `feature-a`:\n\n```\nmain:       A---B\n                 \\\nfeature-a:        C---D\n                       \\\nfeature-b:              E---F\n```\n\nYou want only `E` and `F` on top of `main`:\n\n```bash\ngit rebase --onto main feature-a feature-b\n```\n\nResult:\n\n```\nmain:       A---B\n                |\\\nfeature-a:      | C---D\n                 \\\nfeature-b:        E'---F'\n```\n\n`E` and `F` (the commits after `feature-a`) are replayed onto `main`. `C` and `D` are left out. A plain `git rebase main` on `feature-b` would have carried `C` and `D` along too.\n\n## Another common use: dropping commits\n\nTo remove commits `C` and `D` from the middle of a branch `A-B-C-D-E-F`:\n\n```bash\ngit rebase --onto B D\n```\n\nThis replays everything after `D` (`E` and `F`) onto `B`, so `C` and `D` are gone.\n\n**Tip:** Rebasing rewrites commit hashes. Only do it on branches that no one else has based work on, or warn your collaborators before you force-push."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7608, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16975, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7608}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "75847d75-5011-4128-88c9-9a48b5624415", "uuid": "082af657-81d3-4d24-9a38-2ea80f118b6a", "timestamp": "2026-10-02T00:35:18.060Z", "request_id": "req_011CfcUNkwgBN9VeBWPwYJLn"}
